Z||000000||VISCERAL||07-13-99||10:45 AM||||THEY'RE AT MOST AUTOPARTS STORES.<br>THEY'RE THE 10MM VERSION-THERE'S 2 VERSIONS OF THE 10 MM FOR THE BRAKES I'LL LET YOU ALL KNOW. UNSURE WHAT WILL FIRT THE SLAVE YET. I'LL KEEP YOU ALL UPDATED.<p>I'M GONNA PUT IT ON MY BRAKES SOON, BUT IT ALSO SHOULD MAKE IT EASIER TO BLEED THE CLUTCH SLAVE CYLINDER, AND AFTERWARDS DO THE V8 ARCHIE METHOD TO PUSH THE ROD BACK IN WHILE THE VALVE IS OPEN THEN HAVE A FRIEND CLOSE IT AFTERWARDS.
Z||000001||Eric||07-13-99||05:30 PM||||The best way to bleed the clutch is with a pressure bleeder.  You just attach it to the slave bleeder screw, create a vacume, and it will suck all the air out of the system.  I got mine for about $40 at Napa.
Z||000002||theogre||07-14-99||07:55 AM||||that's a vacuum bleeder E...... Nice thing with these is that you can use the little hand pump for other vacuum device work as well.<p>A pressure bleeder has a tank you fill with fluid and pressurize. It uses an adaptor that attaches to the master cyl and forces the fluid in. Pressure bleeders are expencive, and unless used very carefully a bit dangerous.
Z||000003||Patrick||07-14-99||04:06 PM||||<br>Just to confuse the whole lot of you, I converted my vacuum bleeder to a pressure bleeder and I've found that it's the only way that I can effectively bleed my clutch.<p>  
Z||000004||PaUL||07-15-99||08:07 PM||||I use my own 1 man method to bleed my clutch. This is an ultra cheap method using no special tools. I wedge a deep socket between the clutch pedal and bracket to keep the pedal pushed down. I use an 18mm socket. Then I loosen the nipple and tighten. I repeat this several times always making sure theres fluid in the master.
Z||000005||PAWPAW||07-22-99||04:55 AM||||The only way I could successfully bleed the clutch was by the gravity method, reccomended by a local Saturn dealer mechanic.
Z||000006||Patrick||07-22-99||11:25 PM||||<br>PAWPAW, whatwhat is the "gravity method"?<p>
Z||000007||VISCERAL||07-23-99||09:23 AM||||I believe the best way to bleed the slave cynder is V8 ARCHIE'S way- to take out the bleeder valve and have a pair of needle nose lock pliers to grab the end of the slave pushrod and pull back into the body all the way then hold it while someone tightens the valve back up. Great results and my clutch fully disengages and feels real firm too. At least I'll use the 1 man bleeder valves for my brakes. 
